# roster.conf — CITY-LOCAL config for switchyard-ops. NOT versioned in this repo.
#
# Install to:  $GC_PACK_STATE_DIR/roster.conf
#              i.e. <city>/.gc/runtime/packs/switchyard-ops/roster.conf
#
# That `packs/` segment is load-bearing. Every order resolves this file through
# GC_PACK_STATE_DIR, which gc sets to <city>/.gc/runtime/packs/<pack-name>; a
# roster.conf one directory up (<city>/.gc/runtime/switchyard-ops/) is read by
# nothing. To confirm you picked the right directory, look for the marker files
# the orders themselves write — nightly-retro.unconfigured, pool-spawn.assigned-
# last, loop-health.missing-last. roster.conf belongs beside those. (A city whose
# every rig is cut over to CLOUD_POOL_RIGS below never writes pool-spawn.assigned-
# last at all — cloud dispatch keeps no cursor — so use one of the other two.)
#
# Installing it anywhere else fails SILENTLY and identically to not installing
# it at all: sy_load_conf sources nothing, the roster comes back empty, and
# intake-sweep nudges no coordinator and exits 0. There is no error and no
# alarm — the dispatch lane simply goes quiet.
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL. With no roster.conf at all, switchyard-ops
# derives its roster from `gc agent list --json`: every agent with
# pool.min >= 1 that is not suspended. That covers the normal case.
#
# You only need this file for things gc config cannot express.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PINNED_EXTRA — agents that must stay alive but must NOT be reconciler-pinned.
#
# The singleton-alias hazard: an agent whose alias is held by a long-lived
# manual session. Setting min_active_sessions=1 does NOT keep that session
# alive — the reconciler only counts sessions it spawned — so it mints a
# SECOND session that fights the alias. Such agents stay pool.min=0, and
# loop-health keeps them alive by waking the alias instead.
#
# Format: rig/agent
#   Liveness is resolved through `gc session list --json`, joining on the
#   session's `.template` (the agent's qualified name). Give the agent name and
#   nothing else — gc is asked what it named the session.
#
#   The legacy ":tmux-prefix" suffix is still ACCEPTED and IGNORED. It used to
#   name a tmux pane-name prefix to grep for, which never matched anything: gc
#   does not name a pane after the agent's base name (agent
#   `<rig>/gastown.witness` runs in a pane called `<rig>--gastown__witness`), so
#   every agent read dead on every cycle and loop-health mailed the mayor every
#   30m about a healthy city. There is no longer a prefix to override; drop the
#   suffix when you next touch this file.
#
# config-drift ASSERTS these are not min=1, and mails the mayor if they are.
#
# PINNED_EXTRA="<rig>/<coordinator>:<tmux-prefix>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RETRO_AGENT — which agent drafts the nightly cross-project report.
# Unset => nightly-retro no-ops (and says so, once per day, to the mayor).
#
# RETRO_AGENT="<rig>/<coordinator>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COORDINATORS — override the set nudged by intake-sweep.
# Unset => the liveness roster (derived pins + PINNED_EXTRA), which is normally
# exactly the coordinator set: pinned, long-lived, one per rig.
#
# COORDINATORS="<rig>/<coordinator> <rig>/<coordinator>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RIG_PROJECTS — explicit rig-to-project bindings, when the rig NAME is not the
# project SLUG.
#
# Unset (the default) means a rig is matched to the project whose slug EQUALS the
# rig name, which is what this pack has always done and what still happens for
# every rig not named here. This file staying absent changes nothing.
#
# Set it when slug equality cannot be satisfied or is not wanted:
#
#   * THE NAMES SIMPLY DIFFER. Rig `switchyard-forge` drives a project slugged
#     `forge`. Nothing is wrong with either name, they are just not equal, and the
#     rig resolves to NOTHING until one of them is bound or renamed.
#   * THE SLUG IS AMBIGUOUS. A slug present in two of this token's workspaces
#     (`ideapop` is) cannot be resolved by rig name alone and is deliberately
#     treated as not-found. Naming the rig here says which workspace is meant.
#
# WHY YOU WANT THIS RATHER THAN RENAMING THE PROJECT. Renaming the cloud slug to
# match the rig works, but it makes a cloud-side identifier permanently
# load-bearing for a local rig name, and it leaves the same trap armed for the
# next rig added under a name nobody thought to reserve as a slug.
#
# HOW BADLY IT FAILS WHEN UNSET AND UNMATCHED — measured 2026-08-11 on a city
# whose forge rig had no matching slug: FIVE lanes (answerer, dupe-scout,
# golden-journey, judge, intake-triage) resolved to nothing simultaneously. The
# lane-queue probes FAIL OPEN, so those rigs were still spawned into and each
# session exited IDLE for want of a project to scope to — every lane reporting as
# running while doing nothing. Cloud-pool dispatch (CLOUD_POOL_RIGS) fails CLOSED
# on the same lookup and spawns nothing instead. One binding fixes both paths.
#
# The value is the same `<tenant-slug>/<project-slug>` a scope takes, NOT a bare
# project slug — the tenant is what disambiguates a slug shared across workspaces,
# so leaving it off would reintroduce the ambiguity this key exists to resolve.
# A rig named here whose target this token cannot reach is an operator error, not
# a fallback; see sy_project_for_rig in assets/lib/switchyard-api.sh.
#
# Format: space-separated <rig>=<tenant-slug>/<project-slug>
#
# RIG_PROJECTS="switchyard-forge=software-factory/forge"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD_POOL_RIGS — the rigs cut over to switchyard's CLOUD claim pool.
#
# Unset (the default) means every rig dispatches from its local `bd` ledger, the
# topology pool-spawn was written for. Naming a rig here switches THAT RIG ONLY to
# switchyard's managed claim pool (docs/dispatch-topologies.md, topology A), which
# is a different shape of dispatch rather than a tuning knob:
#
#   - Demand is read from the project's cloud pool over the switchyard API, not
#     from `gc bd list`. The rig's `bd` ledger is not consulted at all.
#   - NOTHING IS DIRECT-ASSIGNED. The cloud claim is atomic and server-side — the
#     spawned brakeman claims for itself over MCP and a second claimant is refused
#     — so there is no hand-off to race, to guard, or to remember. The
#     `pool-spawn.assigned-last` cursor file is not written for a cut-over rig.
#   - A rig still spawns at most one brakeman per cycle and only while its pool is
#     below max_active_sessions. The WIP bound is unchanged.
#
# WHICH PROJECT A RIG MEANS is resolved by matching the rig NAME against a project
# SLUG the pack's token can reach — the same rule the lane queue probes use. A slug
# that exists in more than one workspace is ambiguous and is treated as unreadable
# (below), never guessed.
#
# THE PROBE FAILS CLOSED. If the pool cannot be read — no token, an unreachable
# switchyard, an ambiguous rig name — the rig spawns NOTHING and the mayor is
# mailed. In this topology the pool is the only evidence of demand, so spawning on
# an unread queue would wake an LLM session against a queue nobody looked at, every
# cycle. A confidently EMPTY pool is silent and spawns nothing either.
#
# Needs the pack's switchyard credential (SWITCHYARD_API_TOKEN, or the
# switchyard-mcp CLI's own token file) and SWITCHYARD_BASE_URL for a non-default
# instance — the same two the judge/answerer queue probes already use.
#
# Format: space-separated rig names.
#
# CLOUD_POOL_RIGS="<rig> <rig>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WITNESS_RIGS — the rigs the witness sweep watches (switchyard PRD #357 P1).
#
# Unset (the default) means the witness-sweep order exits immediately: a city
# that never enables it never notices it exists. Naming a rig turns on three
# checks for THAT RIG, every 30 minutes:
#
#   - THE STUCK-WORK DUAL-WRITE. The server already projects `stuck_work` and
#     `ready_to_merge` entries into the project's decision inbox, each with a
#     stable per-episode id. The sweep mails the mayor ONCE per (type, id)
#     episode — publish-gate's mark-after-delivery ledger — so the escalation
#     survives a dead mayor mailbox. It consumes the surface and never
#     re-derives stall predicates: a lapsed lease the reclaim sweep already
#     returned to the pool is absent from the surface and files nothing.
#   - WEDGED WORKER SESSIONS. A session ACTIVE with a stale last_active whose
#     pane holds a queued, unsubmitted prompt (the state a transient API error
#     strands a session in) gets ONE `gc session nudge`; found in the same
#     state on the next cycle it is escalated by mail instead of nudged again.
#     The sweep never kills or resets a session.
#   - A STALLED DISPATCH LOOP. A rig whose cloud claim pool holds claimable
#     beads while no live worker session exists AND the dispatch order's last
#     cycle failed (per the supervisor log) is mailed once per episode, naming
#     `gc order run pool-spawn` as the manual unstick. A loop merely between
#     cycles, or an empty pool, files nothing.
#
# Needs the pack's switchyard credential (SWITCHYARD_API_TOKEN, or the
# switchyard-mcp CLI's own token file) for the first and third checks — the
# same credential the lane queue probes use. The wedge check is gc-only and
# runs regardless. A rig whose name is not its project slug needs a
# RIG_PROJECTS entry, same as every other consumer.
#
# Format: space-separated rig names.
#
# WITNESS_RIGS="<rig> <rig>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONDUCTOR_RIGS — the rigs whose Buzz project channel gets a Conductor
# (switchyard PRD #371).
#
# Unset (the default) means the conductor order exits before it does anything
# a claim lane does — before the api token read, before the session-roster
# read, before any API call — so an unopted city never issues a claim rather
# than taking one and discarding it. (Loading this file, which may seed it from
# this example on first use, happens before that gate — that is the only side
# effect an unopted city performs.)
# Naming a rig turns on a 1-minute lane for THAT RIG:
#
#   - A directive is a Buzz channel message that @-mentions the project's
#     Conductor bot. Switchyard records it once (keyed on the channel event id,
#     so a relay redelivery never queues a second answer) and refuses one whose
#     author fails the channel-authority gate.
#   - The order claims ONE directive per cycle under a 90-second lease and
#     dispatches the `switchyard-ops.conductor` agent into the rig's worktree,
#     scoped to the bound project. The claim is a cross-machine mutex: several
#     cities may run this lane, and exactly one answers.
#   - The answer is banked through switchyard and posted into the directive's
#     THREAD by the outbound notifier, as the project bot. The answering
#     machine never touches the relay and holds no Nostr key — only an
#     `sy_` token.
#
# Liveness is checked BEFORE the claim, deliberately: holding a 90-second lease
# across a session start-up is how two machines end up answering one question.
# A cold city therefore costs one tick before it claims anything.
#
# Needs the pack's switchyard credential (SWITCHYARD_API_TOKEN, or the
# switchyard-mcp CLI's own token file), and the rig's project must be bound to
# a Buzz channel. A rig whose name is not its project slug needs a RIG_PROJECTS
# entry, same as every other consumer.
#
# Optional overrides. The two numeric ones are normalised to bare digits
# rather than pattern-matched — a value like "90s" would otherwise reach
# `jq --argjson`, which prints nothing, and the malformed claim body would be
# REFUSED by the server (claimed_by is required), so the cycle mails
# "directive claim unreadable" and answers nothing until the value is fixed:
#
#   CONDUCTOR_LEASE_SECONDS   claim lease in seconds; default 90. Unset does
#                             NOT defer to the project's configured lease
#                             (project_directive_policy) — the order always
#                             sends the value explicitly, and an explicit ask
#                             wins over the project's policy.
#   CONDUCTOR_ROSTER_TIMEOUT  seconds allowed for the `gc session list` roster
#                             read; default 120. Digits only — a value like
#                             "2m" makes every cycle read as a roster timeout
#                             and the lane never dispatches.
#   CONDUCTOR_AGENT           agent to dispatch (taken as-is, not normalised);
#                             default switchyard-ops.conductor
#
# Format: space-separated rig names.
#
# CONDUCTOR_RIGS="<rig> <rig>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SY_FANOUT_THRESHOLD / SY_FANOUT_ENABLED — epic fan-out (switchyard PRD #372).
#
# A brakeman runs its build plan past the decomposer before building. Above the
# threshold it mints one parent epic bead with a child per plan item, then
# builds each child through the confined harness documented under FAN-OUT
# CHILDREN below — short-lived workers sharing the brakeman's worktree and
# branch, with exactly one PR for the whole tree. At or under the threshold,
# nothing is minted and the build is exactly what it was before. The threshold
# IS the product surface — below it the lane is byte-for-byte unchanged, so a
# city that sets nothing gets today's behaviour.
#
# "Exceeds" is strict: a plan of exactly SY_FANOUT_THRESHOLD items stays serial.
# A non-numeric value falls back to the default rather than refusing to run — a
# typo here must not take the builder lane down.
#
# The decision is reported on EVERY run, fan-out or serial, so a serial build
# past the threshold is never ambiguous between a short plan and a decomposer
# that broke: those runs carry serial_past_threshold=1 and a reason.
#
# SY_FANOUT_ENABLED=0 is the kill switch — every plan builds serially, and says
# so. Reach for it when the box is loaded or the account is near its cap, since
# every child session draws on the same usage limit.
#
# Defaults: SY_FANOUT_THRESHOLD=4, SY_FANOUT_ENABLED=1.
#
# SY_FANOUT_THRESHOLD="4"
# SY_FANOUT_ENABLED="1"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MERGE_STRATEGY is GONE, and setting it now does nothing.
#
# It configured the retired `merge-gate` order, which stamped a merge strategy
# so gastown's refinery would open a PR rather than committing straight to the
# default branch. A gascity city has no refinery and merges nothing on its own —
# the worker opens the PR and a human merges it — so there is no strategy to
# choose. `publish-gate` replaces that order and takes no configuration.
#
# Delete any MERGE_STRATEGY line when migrating a city off the gastown lane.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FAN-OUT CHILDREN — how a decomposed criterion's child beads are executed
# (switchyard PRD #372, crit:cb7409dc0b5a).
#
# Children are run by assets/scripts/fanout-child-run.sh, one invocation per
# child, INSIDE the parent brakeman's worktree on the parent's branch. They are
# deliberately not slung: `gc sling` and `gc session new` would each hand a
# child its own worktree and this pack's `default_sling_formula`, whose publish
# step opens a pull request — and the criterion a fan-out is building has
# exactly one deliverable, so a second PR is a second delivery of it.
#
# SY_FANOUT_CHILD_LAUNCHER — the command line that runs ONE child, with the
#   parent's worktree as its cwd. The brief is on the child's STDIN and its path
#   is in SY_FANOUT_BRIEF; nothing is appended to argv, because the default
#   launcher takes its prompt from an argument when it gets one and would then
#   prompt the child with a bare filename. Default: `claude -p`. An unresolvable
#   launcher is
#   REFUSED loudly rather than skipped, because a fan-out that quietly ran no
#   children is indistinguishable from one that ran them all and found nothing.
#
# SY_FANOUT_CHILD_LAUNCHER="claude -p"
#
# SY_FANOUT_BRIEF_MAX_BYTES — the digest budget for a child's brief. Default
#   4000. It bounds the ITEM only: the identity block and the rules are a floor
#   and are written first, so a tiny budget yields a brief slightly over it
#   rather than a brief missing the rules it exists to carry. A truncated brief
#   says that it was truncated.
#
# SY_FANOUT_BRIEF_MAX_BYTES="4000"
#
# SY_FANOUT_REFUSAL_LOG — where the confinement records what a child tried to
#   do and was refused (opening a pull request, taking a cloud claim, touching
#   the parent's lease). Defaults to a path inside the harness's own work
#   directory. The report line carries the DELTA for that child, not the file's
#   total: the log is opened append-only, so a shared path accumulates across a
#   whole fan-out and a bare count would blame each child for its predecessors.
#
#   Note what the confinement is and is not. The shims refuse loudly and explain
#   themselves, but they are PATH interposition and a child's login shell
#   re-sets PATH from the user profile — so what actually denies a child the
#   ability to publish or claim is the harness withholding the credentials
#   (empty GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN / GITLAB_TOKEN / SWITCHYARD_API_TOKEN, and
#   GH_CONFIG_DIR / SWITCHYARD_CONFIG_HOME pointed at an empty directory).
#
# SY_FANOUT_REFUSAL_LOG="/var/log/switchyard/fanout-refusals.log"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PARENT'S LEASE, across a fan-out (switchyard PRD #372).
#
# A brakeman normally heartbeats its cloud claim between turns. A fan-out takes
# that away: the parent sits in ONE long call while its children build, and a
# blocked session sends nothing. So the fan-out is wrapped in
# `fanout-lease.sh`, which keeps the lease from outside the session and reaps
# the children if the parent dies.
#
# SY_FANOUT_LEASE_SECONDS — the lease requested on every beat. Default 3600,
#   which is the server's cap; anything larger is clamped there.
#
#   Spelling this on every beat is not tidiness, and the bug runs the opposite
#   way to intuition: the server's heartbeat handler starts from a FIVE-MINUTE
#   default and only overrides it when lease_seconds is present. A claim taken
#   at the one-hour cap and then heartbeated without the value is therefore
#   DOWNGRADED to five minutes by the very call meant to keep it alive. A
#   non-numeric value here falls back to 3600 rather than to the empty string,
#   because the empty string is that downgrade.
#
# SY_FANOUT_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL — seconds between beats. Default 900, matching
#   the fifteen-minute floor the build workflow already documents. It is
#   clamped to a third of the lease: an interval at or past the lease cannot
#   renew in time BY CONSTRUCTION, and a third leaves room for two missed beats
#   on a loaded box. A clamp is reported rather than applied quietly, since a
#   silently corrected knob is one nobody ever fixes.
#
# SY_FANOUT_HEARTBEAT_CMD — the command line that sends ONE beat, for a city
#   that wants to route the call its own way. It is invoked with
#   `--bead <id> --agent <ref> --lease-seconds <n>` appended and the same three
#   values exported as SY_FANOUT_BEAD / SY_FANOUT_AGENT /
#   SY_FANOUT_LEASE_SECONDS. Left unset, the built-in beat posts to the bead
#   action endpoint and needs a tenant, a project and a resolvable token.
#
#   When NO beat is possible at all, the fan-out is REFUSED rather than run.
#   That is the safe direction: the fallback is a serial build, where the
#   session heartbeats over MCP itself, and a fan-out with no keeper is exactly
#   the silent lease loss this machinery exists to prevent.
#
#   THE CREDENTIAL SEAM, spelled out because it is the usual reason the
#   built-in refuses: the beat runs in the SESSION'S SHELL, so it needs a token
#   the shell can resolve — SWITCHYARD_API_TOKEN(S) exported, or a
#   `switchyard-mcp` on PATH holding a stored token (`sy_api_token`'s chain). A
#   token that lives only inside an MCP client's own config (a ~/.claude.json
#   server stanza) is injected into the MCP server process and never reaches
#   this shell, so the keeper refuses with reason=no-heartbeat even though the
#   session's switchyard tools all work. That refusal is correct — fix it by
#   exporting a token for the shell, not by skipping the keeper.
#
# SY_FANOUT_REAP_GRACE — seconds between TERM and KILL when reaping a dead
#   parent's children. Default 5.
#
#   The reaping is owned by a supervisor in its own session, NOT by the
#   parent's exit trap: SIGKILL runs no trap, and SIGKILL is what an OOM
#   killer, a `kill -9` and a controller replacing a wedged session all send.
#   The whole process group goes, not just the direct child — the child is an
#   LLM session that spawns compilers and test runners, and leaving those in
#   the shared worktree corrupts the NEXT fan-out rather than merely wasting a
#   core.
#
#   Nothing is filed against the bead when a parent dies. The lease simply
#   stops being renewed and the pool reclaims it on its ordinary expiry sweep,
#   exactly as for any other abandoned claim. The keeper dying with its parent
#   is the point: one that survived would renew switchyard's only mutex on
#   behalf of a worker that no longer exists, and the bead would never come
#   back to the pool at all — invisible from every dashboard, because a
#   heartbeated claim looks perfectly healthy.
#
# SY_FANOUT_LEASE_LOG — optional path; the keeper's report line is appended.
#
# SY_FANOUT_LEASE_SECONDS="3600"
# SY_FANOUT_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL="900"
# SY_FANOUT_HEARTBEAT_CMD=""
# SY_FANOUT_REAP_GRACE="5"
# SY_FANOUT_LEASE_LOG="/var/log/switchyard/fanout-lease.log"

# --- Merge authority (merge-lane + staging-promote, owner directive 2026-08-19) ---
#
# Both lanes are OFF until their rig list is set — merge authority is a
# deliberate per-city, per-rig grant, never a default. The full gates live in
# orders/merge-lane.toml and orders/staging-promote.toml.
#
# MERGE_LANE_RIGS — rigs whose reviewed, CI-green PRs against MERGE_LANE_BASE
#   are merged automatically, one per rig per run, oldest first. Empty = off.
#
# MERGE_LANE_BASE — the base branch the lane feeds. Default "staging".
#
# MERGE_LANE_HOLD_LABEL — a PR carrying this label is never touched by the
#   lane. Default "do-not-merge".
#
# MERGE_LANE_REVIEW_MARKER — the literal text a fallback review comment must
#   carry (posted after the head's last commit) to count as a finished review
#   when no APPROVED review exists. Default "Verdict: APPROVE" — the string the
#   /code-review fallback verdicts already post.
#
# STAGING_PROMOTE_RIGS — rigs whose STAGING_PROMOTE_FROM branch is promoted
#   into the rig default branch via a merged promotion PR whenever it is ahead
#   and the promotion PR's own trial-merge checks are green. Empty = off.
#
# STAGING_PROMOTE_FROM — the branch promoted. Default "staging".
#
# MERGE_LANE_RIGS=""
# MERGE_LANE_BASE="staging"
# MERGE_LANE_HOLD_LABEL="do-not-merge"
# MERGE_LANE_REVIEW_MARKER="Verdict: APPROVE"
# STAGING_PROMOTE_RIGS=""
# STAGING_PROMOTE_FROM="staging"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Review lane (orders/review-sweep.toml) — the fallback reviewer that produces
# the verdicts merge-lane requires, when the repo's standing reviewer is
# absent or rate-limited. OFF until REVIEW_LANE_RIGS is set: posting verdicts
# under the repo's review bar is authority an operator grants deliberately.
#
# REVIEW_LANE_RIGS — rigs whose open PRs (against REVIEW_LANE_BASE, lacking a
#   finished review on their current head) are dispatched to reviewer
#   sessions, one PR per nudge. Empty = off.
#
# REVIEW_LANE_BASE / REVIEW_LANE_HOLD_LABEL — the same gate values as the
#   merge lane; keep them aligned or the two lanes will disagree about which
#   PRs exist. Defaults "staging" / "do-not-merge".
#
# REVIEW_LANE_MARKER — the approve literal the reviewer posts. MUST equal
#   MERGE_LANE_REVIEW_MARKER, or approvals will never merge. Default
#   "Verdict: APPROVE".
#
# REVIEW_LANE_REJECT_MARKER — the reject literal. A comment carrying it on
#   the current head counts as a FINISHED review (the author's move now), so
#   the sweep stops re-dispatching the same head. Default
#   "Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES".
#
# REVIEW_ASSIGNMENT_TTL — seconds a dispatched-but-undelivered assignment
#   suppresses re-dispatch of the same PR head. Default 5400 (one review-sweep
#   interval plus slack for a long review).
#
# REVIEW_LANE_RIGS=""
# REVIEW_LANE_BASE="staging"
# REVIEW_LANE_HOLD_LABEL="do-not-merge"
# REVIEW_LANE_MARKER="Verdict: APPROVE"
# REVIEW_LANE_REJECT_MARKER="Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES"
# REVIEW_ASSIGNMENT_TTL="5400"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rework lane (agents/rework, routed by orders/repair-sweep.toml) — a
# dedicated repair specialist for judge-rejected criteria. By default
# repair-sweep routes repairs to the shared brakeman pool; a rig listed here
# gets them routed to `<rig>/switchyard-ops.rework` instead, whose prompt is
# written for repair (read the rejection first, prior delivery is the
# starting point) rather than for fresh builds. The sweep starts the session
# itself when none is live. Empty = brakeman routing everywhere (the
# pre-existing behaviour).
#
# REWORK_RIGS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security lane (orders/security-scan.toml). OFF until SECURITY_SCAN_RIGS
# names at least one rig — it used to be on-by-default, which made an
# unconfigured provider a weekly "lane idle" mail forever. The list is
# honored per rig (it reaches lane-ensure's rig walk as a filter), matching
# every sibling *_RIGS switch. SECURITY_SCOUT_PROVIDER still selects the
# provider expectation; the readiness gate resolves a wrapped provider's
# real binary (deepseek -> builtin:opencode probes `opencode`).
#
# SECURITY_SCAN_RIGS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SY_NS — the pack's agent namespace: the IMPORT KEY the city registered this
# pack under, which gc uses to qualify every agent name
# (<rig>/<SY_NS>.<agent>). Default "switchyard-ops", the pack's historical
# name. A city that finds `switchyard/switchyard-ops.judge` a mouthful can
# import the pack under a shorter key — [imports.ops] — and set this to match
# IN THE SAME CHANGE: the import key is what actually renames the agents, and
# this is what tells the pack's scripts which names to construct and match.
# One without the other silently blinds every lane (scripts look for agents
# that do not exist). Prose that names the pack itself — mail subjects, the
# validate-sweep rationale — deliberately keeps the pack name.
#
# SY_NS="switchyard-ops"
